        "The moving parts, verified:\n\n```bash\nnpm install express              # adds to dependencies with ^range\nnpm install -D vitest            # devDependencies — build/test only\nnpm ci                           # CLEAN install EXACTLY from the lockfile\n                                 # (CI/production — never 'install' there)\nnpm outdated                     # what's behind its range\nnpm ls left-pad                  # WHY is this in my tree (the path)\nnpm dedupe                      # flatten duplicate versions where compatible\n\nnpm publish                      # ships 'files' + package.json\nnpx playwright install          # run without installing globally\n```\n\nSemver ranges (the resolution rules):\n```text\n^1.2.3  → >=1.2.3 <2.0.0   (minor+patch allowed — the npm default)\n~1.2.3  → >=1.2.3 <1.3.0   (patch only)\n1.2.3   → exactly          (the lockfile uses exact versions)\n^0.x.y   → careful: 0.x treats x like a major (breaking allowed in 0.x)\n```\nThe semver CONTRACT: MAJOR = breaking, MINOR = feature (backwards-compatible), PATCH = fix — and the trust problem: a dependency CAN break you without touching major (the reason lockfiles + npm ci exist: the lockfile pins the RESOLVED tree; 'npm install' inside a project honors the lock unless ranges drifted — but fresh installs without a lock resolve today's latest).\n\nThe dependency TYPES (each answers a different question): dependencies = 'my code imports this at RUNTIME' (bundled/shipped); devDependencies = 'my workflow needs this' (test runners, linters, bundlers — excluded for library CONSUMERS installing my package); peerDependencies = 'I integrate with the consumer's copy of this' (a React component package peer-depends on react — the consumer's React, not a nested one: singletons like React, plugins, and CSS-in-JS libraries are the classic cases — the version RANGE communicates compat: ^18); optionalDependencies = install if possible, skip silently (platform-specific binaries). engines (the node version constraint — warns, doesn't block unless engine-strict) and workspaces (the monorepo protocol: one root lockfile for all packages).\n\nThe lifecycle scripts (the hooks that build the ecosystem): prepare (runs on pack AND on consumer install — the transpile-on-install pattern for src-only packages), preinstall/postinstall (native builds — node-gyp, playwright's browser download), prepublishOnly (the publish gate: run tests before shipping). The security layer: npm audit (known-CVE matching against the tree), npm overrides (force a transitive version — the quarantine tool while waiting for upstream), and npm ls + why to trace who pulls what.",
