      language: "js",
      speakable:
        "The two models, side by side:\n\n```text\nWEBPACK (bundle-centric):\n  dev:    build ONE bundle (incremental + HMR patches it live)\n  prod:   resolve DAG → split chunks → tree-shake → minify →\n          emit hashed assets + sourcemaps\n  strength: mature plugin graph, fine chunk control\n  cost:    startup scales with graph size (the thing Vite attacks)\n\nVITE (native-ESM dev + Rollup prod):\n  dev:    serve FILES as native ESM — the BROWSER requests modules\n          on demand; only requested modules get transformed (esbuild:\n          TS/JSX 10-100x faster than js compilers)\n          → startup instant; HMR = the one changed module (bounded blast\n            radius — edits feel local)\n  prod:   Rollup bundle: split, tree-shake, hash — same output class\n          as webpack, modern defaults\n```\n\nThe concepts that transfer between both (the vocabulary): ENTRY (the graph root — webpack entries; Vite's index.html); the DEPENDENCY GRAPH (every import resolved — alias/@, node_modules, asset imports); CODE SPLITTING (dynamic import() creates an async CHUNK — route-level/lazy-section boundaries: the user downloads the dashboard code when they navigate there, not at first paint); TREE-SHAKING (ESM's STATIC structure — `import { a }` proves 'b is unimported' → dropped; CommonJS require is dynamic → cannot be proven, stays — the reason libraries ship ESM builds); SOURCE MAPS (the transformed-code → original mapping — DevTools shows YOUR source while running the bundle); HMR (the dev loop: module replaced in place, state-preserving where the runtime accepts it — React Fast Refresh); PLUGINS/LOADERS (the transform pipeline at every step — the extension model).\n\nThe production checklist (what a good build does, name the parts): split by route (and vendor) so pages load their own code; preload the current route's chunk (modulepreload) and lazy the rest; content-hash filenames ([name]-[hash].js — immutable caching, the cache-busting mechanism); minify (terser/esbuild) + targeted syntax (browserslist); and measure — a bundle analyzer (visualize the chunks; the 'why is this 400kb' answer).",
